  going to look at each of the row groups  and look at the max value in each of the  pro groups and we're only going to grab  a row group that has a max value that's  greater than 1070 because if the id max  value for given row group is not greater  than 1070 we know that it doesn't  contain any data that's relevant for our  query  so in this first row group up here we  can see that the max id value is 946.  so none of that data is relevant for our  query we're looking for ids greater than  1070.  in this second row group we can see the  max id value is 987.  so we can see that  that's not greater than 1070 so  that rare group also does not have any  data that's relevant for our particular  query  now in this third row group we can see  the max id value is 1080  uh so that does have a max value  uh that's greater than 1070. so that  third row group does have data that's  relevant for a query now  one thing to note is  you can't  cherry pick which data in a row group  gets sent to the cluster  you need to send the whole row group  so  the entire row group is sent including  the data that's not relevant so  we need to perform both the predicate  pushdown filtering and it's still going  to give us some data that's irrelevant  and you need to filter that out on the  cluster side  um hopefully that gives you some  intuition around the predicate push down  filtering  which is another extraordinarily  powerful feature of parquet files  uh  okay  continuing on i'm sure you're convinced  already that that parquet is better than  csv so these these  extra reasons are just extraneous at  this point uh but i'll give them to you  anyways  um  so file compression  so  parquet files can be compressed easier  than csv files parquet files are  columnar file format  versus csv files which are robase file  format  so row base files store their data in  this manner on the left  whereas columnar files store their data  similar to the manner on the right it's  kind of like a trend transposed version  of a robase file  now columnar file formats store similar  data next to each other so we can see  like the one two and three are all right  next to each other  uh compression algorithms really like  that  when similar data is near each other  compression algorithms can do their  magic better than they can normally  and that's why  columnar file formats are more  compressible and will result in smaller  file sizes  let's uh look at the recaps of a little  experiment i ran just to confirm this  so what i did was i created a 315  million road data set  and wrote out some gzipped par k files  and then wrote that same exact data set  out as gzip csv files  uh  so i wrote out 521 parquet files and 521  csv files  using the same compression algorithm  just to make sure it's a good  apples-to-apples test  the parquet data was in total  6.3 gigs  and the csv data was 7.5 gigs so the csv  data set was 19 bigger in this case  this shows of course that columnar files  are more suppressable  and this data was written in a public uh  s3 bucket so coiled data sets  time series 1990s  so if you'd like to check this out you  can feel free to query that data  yourself  um  this notebook used to create those data  sets is in the  coiled resources github uh repo if you  want to check it out i'm just gonna show  you the snippet just because this is  cool  this desk data sets uh time series has  been a godsend for me for  experimentation purposes it's such a  nice useful little data set um  so we can see that  uh  we generate this data set it has 315  million rows of data  it has 521 partitions which is the 521  files that get written out  and we're writing those out as part k  files  it looks like there's some q a just  going to take a look um  uh i'm going to take a look at these  questions later  sorry everybody uh  okay  my brain can't handle being distracted  sorry  i will i will answer those questions  though let me try to rush through the  rest of this i'm prepared to answer  those questions  um  okay  so  reason number four why  parquet is better than csv  is that parquet files contain schema  metadata in the file footer  um  okay  so when you're reading a parquet file  there's metadata in the file itself  so the schema in particular is in the  file footer so when you read in the  parquet file you know all of the column  names and all the column types it's just  already there there's no guessing  there's no reason to do inference or  specifying anything so it's just  wonderful  that is not the case with csv files  with csv files you either need to  manually specify the schema  or you need to infer the schema and by  schema i mean the column names and the  column types  manually specifying the schema  is error prone right we don't users  don't necessarily do it correctly  and it can be really tedious uh let's  say your data set has like a thousand  columns who wants to manually specify  that  uh  inferring the schema is equally  unappealing uh you need to either  take a sample of the data and try to  infer it based on the sample  that's quicker but error prone right  you're not necessarily going to infer  properly  or  you need to  infer based on the entire data set which  is expensive that's like a big data  analysis  let's take a look at some of these pain  points  i'm only have 10 minutes left so i need  to rush here  um  so  let's take a look at reading a csv into  a das data frame  so  let's suppose we have this csv data with  first name and age columns  we read it into a dash data frame and we  can see that the d types are correctly  inferred in this case right we didn't  manually specify it so that means that  desk is going to use inference  we can see we've correctly inferred  first name to be an object and the age  to be an integer so in that case we're  sitting pretty  let's look at a case when this doesn't  work out well um  so we have another data set  we have the same first name and age  columns but in this case we