ce if you're dealing with  cloud-based storage this can be a bit of  a moot point because  a csv um that's in an object store like  s3 is immutable in any case  okay  so  i'm hoping at this point you're  convinced that parquet is better than  csv  uh so if you have all these csv files  how do you how do you convert them to  parquet it turns out that's really easy  um  you can just read a bunch of csv files  into a desk data frame and then write  them out as parquet  you can also perform this operation with  spark there's a lot of different  technologies that make this super easy  uh if i am given a csv data lake the  first thing i will do is convert it to  parquet before doing anything else  because  i strongly dislike working with csv  so  the future  what is coming down the pipeline  well  there are additional exciting  technologies that are even improving  upon parquet files  delta lake is an abstraction on top of  parquet that uses  parquet plus a transaction log to add  some additional functionality iceberg is  really another  columnar file store that's really  exciting  i  have given talks previously on delta  lake so feel free to google those if  you're interested in learning more  uh okay  i have a few minutes left for questions  i wanted to leave that  i'm a pretty easy person to get in touch  with  my email address is powers at coil.io  feel free to email me about distributed  computing file formats  desk spark  international travel i love traveling  currently in brazil  uh  i am in the desk slack which is a  wonderful place to be so feel free to  join  i'm also on the desk reddit i actually  created that also created the desk a  linkedin group  so  feel free to ping me in any of those uh  channels i love chatting with the  community  and helping people out so  hopefully you found this presentation  useful and i will take some questions  all right thank you very much matthew  oh parque and csv that's a great topic  and we have many many questions so  let's